WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.688
Inside one session: clearing and compaction.

2
00:00:02.968 --> 00:00:10.307
When a single long session outgrows its useful window, you have two server-side tools on the Claude API, both in beta.

3
00:00:10.587 --> 00:00:24.560
Tool-result clearing (context editing, beta header context-management-2025-06-27, strategy clear_tool_uses_20250919 ).

4
00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:43.229
Once the input passes a trigger (by default 100,000 input tokens), the API clears the oldest tool results, keeps the most recent few (by default 3 tool uses), and replaces each cleared result with placeholder text so the model knows something was removed.

5
00:00:43.509 --> 00:00:55.285
You can exclude tools whose results must stay, optionally clear the tool inputs too, and set clear_at_least so that clearing happens only when it frees enough tokens to justify breaking the prompt cache.

6
00:00:55.565 --> 00:00:59.000
The response reports what was cleared in context_management.

7
00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:00.389
applied_edits.

8
00:01:00.669 --> 00:01:13.192
Anthropic’s context engineering post calls tool-result clearing one of the safest, lightest-touch forms of compaction: once a result is deep in the history, the agent rarely needs to see it raw again.

9
00:01:13.472 --> 00:01:18.229
Compaction replaces older turns with a summary that Claude writes on the server.

10
00:01:18.509 --> 00:01:33.699
The compaction docs now describe three ways to do it: compaction on demand (you request a summary and swap it in), compaction at a token threshold (the API does it inside an ordinary request), and your own summarizer on the client.

11
00:01:33.979 --> 00:01:38.309
Each has its own page and beta header, so read the overview before you choose.

12
00:01:38.589 --> 00:01:40.211
The two are complementary.

13
00:01:40.491 --> 00:01:44.992
Clearing is mechanical and loses only content the agent can fetch again.

14
00:01:45.272 --> 00:01:52.931
Compaction can preserve decisions and open questions in prose, but a summary can drop the one detail a later step needs.

15
00:01:53.211 --> 00:01:59.653
Anthropic’s advice for writing a compaction prompt is to maximize recall first, then trim for precision.

16
00:01:59.933 --> 00:02:06.909
Both cost you the prompt cache at the point of the edit, so they are worth doing in large steps, not a few tokens at a time.
